   By starting with `\r` (a whitespace character), we force the engine to engage the `^\s+` anchor and begin consuming characters greedily. Lodash's old regex treats `\s` as a complex Unicode whitespace class (`[ \t\r\n\v\f\u00A0...]`). When concatenated in large blocks, V8's regex engine historically struggled to optimize transitions between different internal whitespace categories during global replacement. This causes the NFA (Non-deterministic Finite Automaton) simulation path to activate, leading to repeated state re-evaluations and exponential backtracking attempts that easily surpass the `30000` backtrack limit under Node.js's experimental regex engine flags.

